Internal mic: unmute only after its path has settled (no thump at a capture start)

When a capture starts, the internal mic's path powers up and its samples jump to full scale and drain back over
about half a second (a DC step through a high-pass filter): every recording started with a thump that clipped
its first 0.1 s (MacBookPro13,2, mic at 85 %: 100 ms at 0 dBFS, back to the noise floor after ~0.52 s). It only
happens when the mic was powered down (a second recording within PipeWire's suspend delay is clean).

The driver unmutes the input amp (node 0x22) right after starting the stream. Muting it for the first 0.54 s
of a recording showed no step at the unmute: the step happens before the amp. So keep the amp muted after a
capture start and unmute from delayed work, intmike_settle_ms later (default 500; 0 = unmute at once as
before). The first half second of a recording is silence instead of the thump. The work is cancelled when the
capture stops and when the codec is freed.

The work is also cancelled in cirrus_apple.h, the file kernels from 6.17 on build (patch_cirrus_apple.h is for
older kernels): without it a pending unmute could outlive the codec when the driver is removed.

For github.com/davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro (and the egorenar fork), after
0001-internal-mic-gain-from-mixer.patch. Applied by scripts/install-sound.sh.
